1
00:00:00,240 --> 00:00:03,220
So, um, picture this universal administrative problem.

2
00:00:03,520 --> 00:00:08,300
A school changes its name, right? But weeks later, other government offices are still using the old one.

3
00:00:08,640 --> 00:00:09,740
Yeah. Which is super common.

4
00:00:09,920 --> 00:00:16,620
Right. And why does it happen? Because each office kept its own duplicated copy, which just leads to completely fragmented and siloed data.

5
00:00:16,860 --> 00:00:19,280
Exactly. Well, welcome back to the Deep Dive.

6
00:00:19,520 --> 00:00:28,920
Today, we're unpacking how to solve this data duplication for good, for you, the officials running these systems, and we're using the fictional country of Progressa as our guide here.

7
00:00:29,420 --> 00:00:37,080
Yes. Progressa gives us a great model for exactly how organizations should handle the records they keep, and the key here is, well,

8
00:00:37,720 --> 00:00:43,080
you can stop this duplication before a software developer even draws the first screen of a new system.

9
00:00:43,400 --> 00:00:47,740
Wait, you're saying we can stop government data duplication before writing a single line of code?

10
00:00:48,160 --> 00:00:50,840
Yeah, yeah. Because that sounds, I mean,

11
00:00:51,740 --> 00:00:57,800
a bit of a stretch. Usually these data silos are deeply entrenched IT problems, not something you just fix on a whiteboard.

12
00:00:58,080 --> 00:01:01,520
It sounds counterintuitive, I know, but it actually starts with language.

13
00:01:02,160 --> 00:01:06,480
We have to strictly define the records your service keeps at an atomic level.

14
00:01:06,780 --> 00:01:07,580
Okay, like what?

15
00:01:07,880 --> 00:01:11,000
Take Progressa's quality authority, which handles licensing.

16
00:01:11,580 --> 00:01:15,480
They track four very specific things. Number one, an institution.

17
00:01:16,120 --> 00:01:17,700
Number two, an application.

18
00:01:18,360 --> 00:01:21,360
Number three, a license. And number four, a decision.

19
00:01:21,860 --> 00:01:30,300
Oh, so you have to define a license down to its barest elements, like, um, one permission granted to one institution on one day.

20
00:01:30,760 --> 00:01:36,580
Precisely. If you leave it vague, two different software developers will build two different databases.

21
00:01:37,260 --> 00:01:42,040
And then, boom, you have duplicated data again, which is really costly to change later.

22
00:01:42,680 --> 00:01:45,120
Right. That is the exact mechanism of failure.

23
00:01:45,820 --> 00:01:49,400
If two officials define a record differently, that's not an IT problem.

24
00:01:49,900 --> 00:01:53,420
It's a business question that the person running the service has to resolve immediately.

25
00:01:54,140 --> 00:01:54,500
Got it.

26
00:01:55,220 --> 00:01:57,820
So once everyone actually agrees on the definitions,

27
00:01:58,680 --> 00:02:01,020
how do we stop the duplication at a system level?

28
00:02:01,500 --> 00:02:05,920
By ensuring that for every single fact in the system, we apply one of three answers.

29
00:02:06,360 --> 00:02:10,460
Number one, we keep it. Number two, we take it from another body.

30
00:02:10,880 --> 00:02:13,260
Or number three, we must not hold it at all.

31
00:02:14,020 --> 00:02:14,920
Okay, let's ground that.

32
00:02:15,600 --> 00:02:22,240
Think about how Progressa handles data. When a school applies for a license, the quality authority obviously keeps the school's name.

33
00:02:22,620 --> 00:02:25,540
That's fact number one. They keep the institution's name.

34
00:02:25,980 --> 00:02:27,080
Yes, exactly.

35
00:02:28,020 --> 00:02:30,880
But then look at fact number two, the applicant's identity.

36
00:02:32,380 --> 00:02:33,080
They don't keep that.

37
00:02:33,500 --> 00:02:34,300
They never copy it.

38
00:02:35,160 --> 00:02:42,300
The applicant's identity is checked against the national identity system, so the quality authority takes it from another body, keeping an identifier

39
00:02:42,800 --> 00:02:44,560
but never the actual national ID number.

40
00:02:45,120 --> 00:02:45,600
Oh, I see.

41
00:02:46,280 --> 00:02:50,580
So the identity system handles who you are, the quality authority handles what you do.

42
00:02:52,040 --> 00:02:54,000
So what about fact number three? Where does,

43
00:02:54,680 --> 00:02:58,920
say, the Ministry of Education fit in with the institution's record at the ministry?

44
00:02:59,660 --> 00:03:04,400
The ministry simply reads the institution's record from the quality authority's register in real time.

45
00:03:05,180 --> 00:03:06,460
They don't have their own file on it.

46
00:03:06,860 --> 00:03:08,760
Nope. They only keep the register number.

47
00:03:09,480 --> 00:03:13,040
Because a ministry with no local copy cannot possibly drift out of step.

48
00:03:14,320 --> 00:03:16,460
Yeah, that makes so much more sense. It's less like, um,

49
00:03:17,180 --> 00:03:22,220
syncing a digital address book where you might end up with rekeyed data and more like checking a passport.

50
00:03:22,940 --> 00:03:24,060
The data stays with the source.

51
00:03:24,820 --> 00:03:25,260
Exactly.

52
00:03:25,940 --> 00:03:29,120
And this approach aligns perfectly with the GovStack specifications.

53
00:03:29,700 --> 00:03:32,800
Right, the global initiative for standard digital government services.

54
00:03:33,100 --> 00:03:33,220
Yeah.

55
00:03:33,940 --> 00:03:40,840
The GovStack digital registry specification states that a register is a trusted, authoritative, single source of truth.

56
00:03:41,140 --> 00:03:44,740
And other services just check that a record exists or read a value from it.

57
00:03:45,160 --> 00:03:45,440
Correct.

58
00:03:46,100 --> 00:03:52,080
And then the GovStack identity specification states that the person signs in and approves exactly which facts are shared.

59
00:03:52,480 --> 00:04:01,900
Knowing those standards is great, but, um, how do you actually guarantee a software supplier codes it this way so you aren't locked into one supplier with a system that doesn't talk to anything else?

60
00:04:02,520 --> 00:04:04,360
You do it through a process called a read back.

61
00:04:04,940 --> 00:04:12,680
Before accepting the system design, the software supplier literally reads the records in plain sentences aloud to the official who runs the service.

62
00:04:13,140 --> 00:04:15,340
Wait, they literally read it out loud to the official?

63
00:04:15,600 --> 00:04:16,060
Yes, they do.

64
00:04:16,500 --> 00:04:20,500
That sounds so low tech, but I guess it forces everyone to speak the exact same language.

65
00:04:20,839 --> 00:04:21,180
It does.

66
00:04:22,019 --> 00:04:29,440
If the supplier reads something fundamentally wrong, like, say, an institution may hold one license of each kind, the official stops them.

67
00:04:30,020 --> 00:04:37,840
Because in reality, an institution whose provisional license was canceled might need to apply again, so they fix the logic before coding begins.

68
00:04:38,140 --> 00:04:41,380
Precisely. Because if a fact has no clear owner,

69
00:04:42,080 --> 00:04:45,120
it will simply be kept by whoever builds the first screen that needs it.

70
00:04:45,580 --> 00:04:45,820
Wow.

71
00:04:46,740 --> 00:04:48,180
Which is why for every single fact,

72
00:04:48,840 --> 00:04:51,340
you must ask which of those three answers it has.

73
00:04:51,800 --> 00:04:56,840
And if you don't ask, you end up with the exact fragmented siloed data we started with.

74
00:04:57,680 --> 00:04:59,740
It really changes how you look at building systems.

75
00:05:00,140 --> 00:05:01,140
It definitely does.

